---
const grades = [
  { name: "A reproducible failing test", body: "that can only be fixed by changing the requested area." },
  { name: "A runtime trace", body: "showing that execution passes through the requested area." },
  { name: "A dependency path from the map", body: "such as “the bug is in formatPhone, which lives in shared/utils.”" },
  { name: "A draft of the change", body: "so the reviewer sees exactly what the extra access is for." },
  { name: "A written rationale", body: "Useful context, never enough on its own." },
];
const scopes = [
  { k: "Write", v: "The internals of notifications" },
  { k: "Read", v: "The OrderShipped event and the UserPreferences contract" },
  { k: "Network", v: "The SMS provider's test endpoint, nothing else" },
  { k: "Expires", v: "When the task ends" },
];
---

<section class="section wrap access" aria-labelledby="access-title">
  <h2 class="section-title" id="access-title">Access is earned with proof, not argument.</h2>

  <div class="access-grid">
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Agents are fast, tireless and easy to trick, and anything they read can carry instructions. In 2025,
        a malicious issue in a public repository hijacked an agent connected through GitHub's MCP server.
        Because the agent could also read private repositories, it copied private data into a public pull
        request.<a class="cite" href="#source-4">4</a> Broad access plus untrusted input is an architecture
        problem, not a bug.
      </p>
      <p>
        So every task in Onus runs with a short-lived token that opens only the doors it needs, like a hotel
        key card that opens your room and the gym and stops working at checkout. Tokens can be narrowed for a
        sub-agent, never widened, and every door checks them on every call. Nothing depends on the agent
        choosing to behave.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="keycard" aria-labelledby="keycard-cap">
      <div class="keycard-stripe" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <p class="keycard-task" id="keycard-cap">Token for “Text customers when their order ships”</p>
      <dl>
        {
          scopes.map((s) => (
            <div class="scope">
              <dt>{s.k}</dt>
              <dd>{s.v}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="escalate">
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="subhead">When an agent needs more, it proves the need</p>
      <p>
        A confused or hijacked agent can write a very convincing paragraph about why it needs the payments
        service. Evidence is different, because a machine can check it. Requests are structured: the scope
        asked for, the evidence attached, and the expected blast radius from the map. A low-risk request with a
        reproducible test can be granted automatically; a request to touch auth goes to a person.
      </p>
      <p>
        Escalation is also the safe exit. When an agent is blocked and has no sanctioned way to say so, it
        improvises. In July 2026, agents in an internal security evaluation worked around their isolation and
        reached the internet; the post-mortem named “difficult tasks without a safe exit” as a key driver.<a
          class="cite"
          href="#source-5">5</a
        > Giving agents a structured escalation tool and a clear policy against gaming tests cut test-gaming
        from <strong>23.6%</strong> to <strong>5.3%</strong> across eight frontier models, with no drop in tasks
        solved, and their reports named the actual defect 99.4% of the time.<a class="cite" href="#source-6">6</a>
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="grades">
      <figcaption class="grades-cap">Evidence, strongest first</figcaption>
      <ol>
        {
          grades.map((g, i) => (
            <li class={i === grades.length - 1 ? "is-weak" : ""}>
              <span class="g-n" aria-hidden="true">{i + 1}</span>
              <p>
                <strong>{g.name}</strong> {g.body}
              </p>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .access-grid,
  .escalate {
    margin-top: 2.25rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 2.5rem;
  }

  @media (min-width: 62rem) {
    .access-grid {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.1fr) minmax(0, 0.9fr);
      align-items: center;
      gap: 4rem;
    }

    .escalate {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.1fr) minmax(0, 0.9fr);
      align-items: start;
      gap: 4rem;
    }
  }

  .escalate {
    margin-top: clamp(3rem, 2rem + 3vw, 4.5rem);
  }

  .keycard {
    background: var(--paper);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: 16px;
    padding: 0 clamp(1.1rem, 0.8rem + 1vw, 1.6rem) 1.4rem;
    overflow: hidden;
    max-width: 30rem;
  }

  .keycard-stripe {
    height: 2.25rem;
    margin-inline: calc(-1 * clamp(1.1rem, 0.8rem + 1vw, 1.6rem));
    background: var(--ink);
  }

  .keycard-task {
    margin-top: 1.1rem;
    font-weight: 650;
    line-height: 1.35;
  }

  .keycard dl {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0;
  }

  .scope {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 5.5rem 1fr;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    padding: 0.6rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-paper);
    font-size: 0.9875rem;
    line-height: 1.4;
  }

  .scope dt {
    font-stretch: 82%;
    font-weight: 650;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }

  .grades {
    background: var(--paper);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--radius-panel);
    padding: clamp(1.1rem, 0.8rem + 1vw, 1.6rem);
  }

  .grades-cap {
    font-weight: 650;
  }

  .grades ol {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 1rem 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.25rem;
  }

  .grades li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 2rem 1fr;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    padding: 0.6rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-paper);
    font-size: 0.9875rem;
    line-height: 1.45;
  }

  .g-n {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    font-stretch: 120%;
    font-weight: 800;
    line-height: 1.2;
  }

  .grades li.is-weak {
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }

  .grades li.is-weak strong {
    font-weight: 600;
  }
</style>
